From 726ba8fa5cbd94264c973a1659c938ce77b2c5d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 17:36:53 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: record who sent the signal that ended a task

The task-exit record held only the wait status, so a recording could say
that SIGKILL ended a task but not who sent it. SIGKILL is never queued with
its siginfo, and a fatal signal nothing catches starts the group exit when
it is sent, so the sender has to be taken at the send.

The recorder keeps one record per thread group in the group leader's state
(the LSM blob of kernel/antinomie/state.h; no stock structure changes),
written and read under the group's siglock, the first one staying:

- kernel/signal.c __send_signal_locked(), after complete_signal(): a send
  that started the group exit gives si_code, si_pid and si_uid as the kernel
  fills them for the target and, for a call sent from process context
  (SI_USER, SI_TKILL, SI_QUEUE), the sending task's global TGID, UID and
  name. SEND_SIG_PRIV is SI_KERNEL without a sender.
- kernel/signal.c get_signal(), before its fatal label: a fatal signal that
  is dequeued (a core-dumping one, one a debugged or ptraced task gets)
  gives the siginfo the task dequeued.
- mm/oom_kill.c __oom_kill_process(), after it logs the kill: its victim's
  record is marked as the OOM killer's, with that log line.

At exit, when the task's wait status names the recorded signal, the
task-exit event carries lifecycle.signal.signo, code, pid, uid and origin,
the sender's pid, uid and name when known, and for an OOM kill
lifecycle.signal.oom and the kernel's line as lifecycle.kernel_log. The
lifecycle.task_exit field stays first and unchanged.

Contract revision 21.
